What is Decitabine?
Decitabine (trade name Dacogen) is a nucleoside analog that acts as a DNA methyltransferase inhibitor, classified as an epigenetic therapy. The drug works by reactivating tumor suppressor genes silenced through DNA hypermethylation. It's primarily indicated for treating myelodysplastic syndromes (MDS) and acute myeloid leukemia (AML), particularly in elderly patients who may not tolerate intensive chemotherapy.

Available in both branded (Dacogen) and generic formulations, Decitabine has become a cornerstone in hematologic malignancy treatment protocols. Its ability to prolong progression-free survival while maintaining manageable toxicity profiles has cemented its position in therapeutic guidelines.

